    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1889, logging pioneer John McReavy platted Union City on Hood Canal's south shore, naming it to celebrate Washington statehood.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.explorehoodcanal.com/stories/unioncity",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Union lies along the southern shore of the Great Bend of Hood Canal, near the mouth of the Skokomish River that flows from the Olympic Mountains.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Union%2C_Washington",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The area once hosted the glamorous artist colony at Olympus Manor, where musicians, actors, and stars gathered before a 1952 fire destroyed the studios.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.explorehoodcanal.com/stories/unioncity",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Union’s Skokomish River was vital to the Skokomish Tribe, providing salmon runs and grasses for weaving baskets.",
